655	    /// Two ops in ONE undo step — `add_media` then `add_background {media}` —
656	    /// which needs the id the FIRST one mints, or the id it declined to mint.
657	    ///
658	    /// An op returns a project and never a value (spec §11: one FFI entry
659	    /// point for every op), so the id is learned by applying `add_media`
660	    /// PURELY, against the document's own handle, and reading it off the
661	    /// resulting snapshot: the core appends, so the last entry is the new one.
662	    /// `ProjectDocument.makeProject(around:)` has done exactly this since M1
663	    /// and this is the first use of the technique outside it, so the reason it
664	    /// is sound is worth writing down here as well: `next_id` is a single
665	    /// counter, so the same op applied to the same base mints the same id
666	    /// every time, and the throwaway project the probe applies to and the one
667	    /// the undo step applies to ARE the same base.
668	    ///
669	    /// **The precedent does not carry the whole way, and the review is what
670	    /// found the gap**: `makeProject(around:)` applies `add_media` to a
671	    /// project built empty two lines above it, where the file cannot already
672	    /// be in the list, and this call site is the case where it can be.
673	    /// `backgroundMedia(for:in:)` is that half.
674	    ///
675	    /// The alternative — `importMedia` and then a second edit — is two undo
676	    /// steps for one command, and a ⌘Z that leaves the picture in the media
677	    /// list is the kind of half-undone state this app does not have anywhere
678	    /// else.
679	    func placeImageBackground(
680	        _ descriptor: MediaDescriptor, into document: ProjectDocument, in window: NSWindow
681	    ) {
682	        let before = document.editedProject.snapshot
683	        let add = ReelOp.addMedia(descriptor: descriptor)
684	        let media: UInt32
685	        do {
686	            // The core's own message for a descriptor it refuses — a picture
687	            // past `MEDIA_DIMENSION_MAX`, a rate with a zero term — rather
688	            // than the sentence below, which would report "could not be
689	            // added" and name none of it. A `try?` here spent the one thing
690	            // the core said.
691	            // The DOCUMENT's handle, not the edited one: `add_media` is a
692	            // document-level op sent unscoped (`ReelOp.isDocumentLevel`), so
693	            // the probe that learns the id it will mint has to be run against
694	            // the same project the real op will land in.
695	            guard let id = try Self.backgroundMedia(for: descriptor, in: document.project) else {
696	                let alert = NSAlert()
697	                alert.messageText = "Could Not Add the Picture"
698	                alert.informativeText =
699	                    "“\(descriptor.path)” could not be added to this project's media list."
700	                alert.beginSheetModal(for: window, completionHandler: nil)
701	                return
702	            }
703	            media = id
704	        } catch {
705	            NSAlert(error: error).beginSheetModal(for: window, completionHandler: nil)
706	            return
707	        }
708	        // `add` stays in the list even when it is an identity: `applying(_
709	        // ops:)` runs every later op after an earlier one that changed
710	        // nothing, so one shape works for both cases and the undo step is
711	        // one either way.
712	        document.performGroupedEdit("Add Background", [add, .addBackground(media: media)])
713	        // The thumbnail the Media tab draws, and the filmstrip the timeline
714	        // draws: the import path generates them after every commit and this is
715	        // a commit like any other. Safe to call repeatedly — a cache whose
716	        // marker matches its file is not rebuilt, so a picture already in the
717	        // list costs the pass a stat and a marker read.
718	        document.generateCaches()
719	        selectClips(addedSince: before, in: document)
720	    }
